  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will be the rule into the coming weekend while \nan upper gyre lingers over the Pacific Northwest. Low levels remain \non the dry side despite southerly flow dominating but moisture in \nthe mid and upper levels will support precipitable water values of a \nleast the 90% percentile of climatology. Forcing for ascent looks to \ncome in a few periods. The first being tonight into Monday as precip \nover southwest Kansas at midday today may brush the area. Chances \nspread east over the remainder of the area Monday night into Tuesday \nas a weak upper wave moves through. The upper flow flattens enough \nfor a weak cold front to move into the area Wednesday night into \nThursday. Instability increases enough to bring some thunder \npotential but shear is rather unidirectional and weak aloft. The \nsouthwest flow brings another wave through Friday night into \nSaturday with the front potentially sparking precip to its north \nmainly in northern areas Thursday night into Friday. Any \nprecipitation through mid-week looks fairly light with somewhat \ngreater chances for moderate amounts late this week into this \nweekend though latest LREF still has chances for half an inch in any \n24-hour period at less than 25%. As for temperatures, precipitation \npotential brings some uncertainty in how warm conditions will get, \nmainly later in the week, but the southerly flow will support above \nnormal conditions, particularly for low temperatures, though this \nforecast.",
